RESULT: PASS -- 9 run(s) completed and none reported a sanitizer problem. The claim is bounded by the coverage below.

COVERED -- runs that completed with NO sanitizer report:
  san_check_e31          mixed 131072 same         
                         E31's in-place sub-block repack, the insert that follows it, deletes of the first and last live records, emptied middle sub-blocks, and the boundary directory re-checked against its own sub-blocks on BOTH arms
  san_check_e31          mixed 131072 grow         
                         the same repack and placement paths run with a growing key length, so suffixes that change size move through them
  san_check_e9_multiblock self-contained pool, 2 trials
                         structural invariants: rebuild, the sub-block sweep, the boundary directory, and empty middle sub-blocks
  san_check_e11_locate   self-contained pool, 3 trials
                         locate against the boundary directory across multi-block groups, including groups whose overflow holds records below every primary key
  san_check_freeze       mixed 131072              
                         rebuild paths: three whole indexes built from one plan at the shipped defaults, including the exact-capacity sub-block cases the allocator produces
  san_bench_long_hyst    mixed 131072 reps=1 same  
                         one overflow-heavy workload: overflow growth, overflow deletion and saved positions going stale under long churn, at ONE repetition on the smallest dataset -- coverage, not a timing run
  san_check_lits_model   all three corpora at their formal sizes
                         the LITS adapter end to end: the third-party HPT's 1,024 allocations and their release, training over 524,288 keys, the region calibration, every key of every real region (524,288 live and 524,288 absent) compared against the source's position arithmetic, the boundary regions at p=0/1/31/32/33/121/123, and the six REFUSAL paths (high byte, embedded NUL, too few keys, key shorter than the prefix, degenerate calibration, predicting on an unusable model)
  san_check_lits_index   mixed 32768, every maintenance path driven
                         the LITS backend through a REAL index: build, update, erase, prefix-mismatch rebuild, split, compaction, overflow insert and erase, and the full scan against a reference map. The corpus is a QUARTER of the formal size because this gate builds TWO indexes and runs seven operation phases over each; the paths it covers do not depend on the key count, and ASan's own overhead is what sets the size here
  san_check_lits_fingerprint all three corpora at their formal sizes
                         the fused fingerprint path read from the frozen model while the LITS adapter reads the same keys, including the negative control that hashes from the model's read offset

NOT COVERED -- this run says NOTHING about:
  * LeakSanitizer, and leaks in general: leak detection is switched off (ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0), so this run says nothing about leaks.
  * the tranco corpus and every dataset/mode/repetition combination the timing sweeps use -- EXCEPT that the E38 gates below do run tranco at its formal 262,144 keys, because their claim is corpus-wide. The timing sweeps are still not covered.
  * code reached only under -DE25_DIAG in the programs that were NOT built with it here (only san_check_e31 carries the counters).
  * the split path as a claim of its own: whatever the listed programs execute is covered, but no run here asserts on a split in its own right.
  * the LITS adapter's NonFinite guard, which is unreachable with `hi > lo` on doubles: the span is at least an ulp of `lo`, so `G * alpha` stays around 1e19. It is a defensive branch and this run does not exercise it (check_lits_model reports it as untriggered).
  * the LITS adapter's empty-region path: all three corpora divide evenly by region_keys, so no empty region exists for these runs to reach.
  * any toolchain or platform other than the one this driver probes; the positive controls prove the detector is live ON THIS MACHINE only.
  * memory errors that neither ASan nor UBSan can see.
  * the scan-contract acceptance (ITEM A): no sanitizer result says anything about scan order, and the two must not be run together into one conclusion.

